{"steps": [
  {"id": "s1", "description": "Call list_documents to see which project documents are available. Identify the candidate material specifications, the calorimeter requirements, and any maintenance, access, or shutdown documentation."},
  {"id": "s2", "description": "Read candidate_specs.md and the other relevant documents from s1. Record the three allowed absorber materials with their numeric properties (for example radiation length, density, and cost, if given). Note any maintenance or access requirements that limit absorber mass, plate handling, or thickness, and record the constraints that apply to the design."},
  {"id": "s3", "description": "Run simulate_resolution for 100 GeV electrons for each of the three candidate materials across plate thicknesses from 2 mm to 10 mm. Use a sensible grid, such as 2, 4, 6, 8, and 10 mm, and add finer points near the best region if the trend needs it. Record the numeric resolution (%) for each material and thickness with record_results, since that tool accepts only numeric values. Keep a clear mapping of which number belongs to which material and thickness."},
  {"id": "s4", "description": "Using the recorded results from s3 and the constraints from s2, filter out any material and thickness combinations that violate the maintenance or access requirements or fall outside the 2 to 10 mm range. Among the remaining options, identify the material and thickness that give the best (lowest) energy resolution at 100 GeV. Check that the choice is not an artifact of a single grid point by confirming that neighboring thicknesses behave consistently. Record the selected resolution value as a number."},
  {"id": "s5", "description": "Submit one recommended absorber material (from candidate_specs.md) and one plate thickness (between 2 and 10 mm) as the final result. Include the simulated resolution for that choice and a brief justification that cites the maintenance constraint check."}
]}
```
